Scheduled Delay-Tolerant Networking

Crystal Arena-Turner

AI : ChatGpt, Claude, Deepseek

Abstract

This chapter develops a rigorous mathematical framework for routing in delay‑tolerant networks (DTNs) where node contacts are entirely predictable from orbital mechanics or pre‑negotiated schedules. We model the network as a time‑varying graph whose edges appear only during known contact windows, each carrying a time‑dependent capacity derived from the Shannon–Hartley theorem and the free‑space path loss. A bundle is a data unit with a creation timestamp, a lifetime, and a destination; forwarding decisions must respect contact start and end times, capacity integrals, and the finite propagation delay imposed by the speed of light. We show that the earliest‑delivery routing problem can be solved exactly by constructing a time‑expanded graph and applying a modified Dijkstra algorithm, and we prove that this construction captures all feasible contact transitions without loss of optimality. The chapter closes by connecting the formal model to real‑world DTN standards such as BPv7, BIBE, and Contact Graph Routing, demonstrating that the mathematics is not merely an abstraction but the operational core of interplanetary networking.

---

1. Introduction

When round‑trip propagation delays exceed a few seconds—as they do for Earth–Moon (≈2.6 s), Earth–Mars (6–44 min), or any deep‑space link—the TCP/IP handshake model becomes unusable. Delay‑Tolerant Networking (DTN) replaces end‑to‑end acknowledgments with hop‑by‑hop custodial storage and scheduled forwarding. The fundamental resource in such a network is not bandwidth alone, but the opportunity to communicate: a contact window during which two nodes are in line‑of‑sight, within antenna range, and have sufficient signal‑to‑noise ratio.

The present treatment strips away all speculative elements and focuses solely on the mathematical skeleton: a formal definition of contacts, capacity, bundles, and the routing problem, together with proofs of correctness for the principal algorithm used in scheduled DTNs—the time‑expanded graph (TEG) construction and the derived shortest‑path search. The definitions are grounded in the physics of electromagnetic propagation and the Shannon–Hartley theorem, but the results hold for any network where link availability and rate are known a priori.

---

2. System Model

2.1 Time and Space

Time is continuous, denoted by t\in\mathbb{R}. The network is a set of nodes V = \{1,2,\dots,N\}. Every pair (i,j) has a Euclidean distance function d_{ij}(t)\ge 0 that varies with the orbital motion of the nodes. The one‑way propagation delay is

\tau_{ij}(t) = \frac{d_{ij}(t)}{c},

where c = 299\,792\ \text{km s}^{-1} is the speed of light in vacuum. Because orbital velocities are much smaller than c, d_{ij}(t) changes slowly; over a contact window of a few hours we may safely treat \tau_{ij} as constant. We therefore associate a single delay \tau_{ij} with each contact opportunity, computed at the start of the window.

2.2 Channel Capacity

Let a transmitter–receiver pair (i,j) be characterised by a carrier frequency (or optical wavelength), antenna gains, and system noise temperature. The maximum error‑free data rate is given by the Shannon–Hartley theorem:

C(t) = B \log_2\!\left(1 + \frac{S(t)}{N}\right) \quad \text{(bits s⁻¹)},

where B is the bandwidth and S(t)/N is the signal‑to‑noise ratio. The received signal power S(t) obeys the Friis free‑space equation

S(t) = \frac{P_t G_t G_r \lambda^2}{(4\pi d_{ij}(t))^2},

with P_t the transmitted power, G_t, G_r the antenna gains, and \lambda the wavelength. Hence

C_{ij}(t) = B \log_2\!\left(1 + \frac{P_t G_t G_r \lambda^2}{(4\pi d_{ij}(t))^2 N}\right).
\tag{1}

In practice the contact planner will discretise the rate to a few constant levels (or an average) for scheduling; our model allows an arbitrary integrable function C_{ij}(t)\ge 0.

2.3 Contact Window

A contact from node u to node v is a tuple

\kappa = (u,\; v,\; t_{\text{start}},\; t_{\text{end}},\; C(\cdot),\; \tau),

where:

· t_{\text{start}} < t_{\text{end}} are the start and end of the window,
· C:[t_{\text{start}}, t_{\text{end}}]\to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is the instantaneous capacity function,
· \tau = \tau_{uv}(t_{\text{start}}) is the (constant) one‑way light‑time during the contact.

During the window the link is available for transmission; outside it no signal can be exchanged. The maximum number of bits that can be delivered over \kappa is the contact volume

V(\kappa) = \int_{t_{\text{start}}}^{t_{\text{end}}} C(s)\,\mathrm{d}s.
\tag{2}

For the network we assume a contact plan \mathcal{C} = \{\kappa_1, \kappa_2, \dots\} that is known perfectly for the whole planning horizon. In real interplanetary systems this plan is generated from orbital ephemerides; the plan is updated periodically but for a given scheduling interval it is immutable.

2.4 Bundles

A bundle is the DTN data unit. It is represented by a tuple

b = (s,\; src,\; dst,\; t_{\text{create}},\; \ell),

where:

· s is the bundle size (bits),
· src, dst \in V,
· t_{\text{create}} is the creation time,
· \ell is the lifetime; the bundle must be delivered by t_{\text{create}}+\ell, or it is discarded.

A node has infinite storage for bundles awaiting forwarding; realistic finite buffers can be layered as a capacity constraint on the node, but for the routing problem we assume that any bundle can be stored for an arbitrary length of time until a suitable contact becomes available.

