3. The Earliest‑Delivery Routing Problem

3.1 Feasible Forwarding over a Single Contact

A bundle b of size s can be transmitted over contact \kappa = (u,v, t_s, t_e, C, \tau) if:

1. The bundle is present at node u at time t_{\text{arr}}\in [t_s, t_e] (the bundle can wait at the sender until the window opens).
2. Transmission begins at some time t_{\text{tx}} \in [\max(t_{\text{arr}}, t_s), t_e] and finishes at t_{\text{tx}} + t_{\text{xfer}}, where t_{\text{xfer}} is the smallest value such that

\int_{t_{\text{tx}}}^{t_{\text{tx}} + t_{\text{xfer}}} C(t)\,\mathrm{d}t \ge s.
\tag{3}

The integral condition ensures that the cumulative capacity during the transmission interval suffices for the whole bundle. Because C(t) is known and piecewise continuous, such a t_{\text{xfer}} exists if and only if

\int_{\max(t_{\text{arr}}, t_s)}^{t_e} C(t)\,\mathrm{d}t \ge s.

If transmission succeeds, the bundle becomes available at node v at time

t_{\text{dlv}} = t_{\text{tx}} + t_{\text{xfer}} + \tau.
\tag{4}

Note that the bundle’s lifetime must not expire before t_{\text{dlv}}.

3.2 Multi‑Hop Path

A path is a sequence of contacts

P = (\kappa_1, \kappa_2, \dots, \kappa_m),

with \kappa_i = (n_{i-1}, n_i, t_{s,i}, t_{e,i}, C_i, \tau_i), where n_0 = src and n_m = dst. For the path to be feasible for a bundle b created at time t_0, there must exist a sequence of arrival times t_0 (at src), transmission start times t_{\text{tx},i}, and delivery times t_{\text{dlv},i} = t_{\text{tx},i} + t_{\text{xfer},i} + \tau_i such that:

· t_{\text{dlv},i} is the arrival time at node n_i,
· t_{\text{arr},i+1} = t_{\text{dlv},i},
· The single‑contact feasibility conditions (3) hold for each \kappa_i with arrival time t_{\text{arr},i},
· t_{\text{dlv},m} \le t_0 + \ell.

The delivery time of the path is t_{\text{dlv},m}. The optimisation problem is:

Given a bundle b and a contact plan \mathcal{C}, find a feasible path that minimises the delivery time, or determine that no path can meet the lifetime constraint.

---

4. Time‑Expanded Graph Construction

The continuous‑time problem can be solved exactly by discretising time at the boundaries of contact windows and at moments when capacity integrals reach a threshold. The standard approach uses a time‑expanded graph (TEG), which we now define formally.

4.1 Event‑Driven Time Discretisation

Let \mathcal{T} be the set of all distinct times that appear as start or end points of any contact in \mathcal{C}. Sort \mathcal{T} = \{t_0, t_1, \dots, t_K\} with t_0 < t_1 < \dots < t_K. Between successive instants t_k and t_{k+1}, the set of active contacts and the capacity functions are constant (we assume capacity is piecewise constant over these intervals; if C(t) varies, the interval is subdivided further until the variation is negligible, or the integral condition can be enforced exactly with an adaptive step). For simplicity of proof we assume C_i(t) is constant on each interval [t_k, t_{k+1}); this is how operational contact plans are built.

4.2 Vertices and Edges

The TEG is a directed graph G^* = (V^*, E^*).

Vertices. For each node u\in V and each time instant t_k\in\mathcal{T} we create a vertex (u, t_k). Intuitively, this vertex represents “the bundle is at node u at time t_k”.

Waiting edges. For every node u and consecutive instants t_k, t_{k+1} we add a directed edge

(u, t_k) \xrightarrow{\text{wait}} (u, t_{k+1})

with cost (edge weight) equal to the waiting time t_{k+1} - t_k. This edge represents the bundle being stored at u during the interval.

Contact transmission edges. For each contact \kappa = (u,v, t_s, t_e, C, \tau), let the interval [t_s, t_e] be covered by a sequence of consecutive TEG instants t_p, t_{p+1}, \dots, t_q (where t_p = t_s, t_q = t_e). The capacity rate C is constant on each sub‑interval [t_k, t_{k+1}). Suppose the bundle is ready to be forwarded at time t_k (where t_s \le t_k < t_e). We compute the smallest m \ge k such that the cumulative capacity from t_k to t_m is at least s:

\sum_{j=k}^{m-1} C \cdot (t_{j+1} - t_j) \ge s.
\tag{5}

If such m exists and t_m \le t_e, the transmission can be completed. The exact finish time within the interval [t_{m-1}, t_m] is obtained by solving for the fractional part, but for the graph we can use a contact completion vertex. A standard technique is to insert, for each contact, a virtual vertex that represents the end of the transmission. However, for the purpose of finding earliest arrival times it suffices to add a single edge per feasible departure instant t_k:

(u, t_k) \xrightarrow{\kappa} (v, t_{\text{dlv}}),

where t_{\text{dlv}} = t_{\text{tx\_end}} + \tau, and t_{\text{tx\_end}} is the exact time when the volume s has been transferred. Because TEG instants are discrete, we create a new vertex (v, t_{\text{dlv}}) if t_{\text{dlv}} does not already belong to \mathcal{T}; we then add that time to the set of instants for the receiving node.

Formally, for each contact \kappa = (u,v, t_s, t_e, C, \tau) and each TEG instant t_k \in [t_s, t_e] we compute t_{\text{finish}} as:

t_{\text{finish}} = t_k + \Delta,

where \Delta is the smallest positive value satisfying

\int_{t_k}^{t_k+\Delta} C \,\mathrm{d}t = s,

with the constraint t_k+\Delta \le t_e. If a solution exists, we create an edge from (u, t_k) to (v, t_{\text{finish}}+\tau) with cost equal to the total elapsed time (t_{\text{finish}}+\tau - t_k). The receiving vertex is added to V^* if not already present.

4.3 Correctness of the TEG Construction

Lemma 1. For any feasible path P in the continuous‑time model that delivers a bundle of size s from src to dst by time T, there exists a corresponding directed path in the TEG from vertex (src, t_0') (where t_0' is the first TEG instant at or after the bundle’s arrival at src) to a vertex (dst, t_{\text{dlv}}) with t_{\text{dlv}} \le T, whose total cost equals the delivery time.

Proof sketch. The continuous path is split into wait intervals and transmission intervals. Wait intervals map to sequences of waiting edges between consecutive TEG instants. Transmission intervals start at a TEG instant t_k (any start time within a constant‑capacity segment can be moved to the nearest earlier TEG instant without increasing finish time, because capacity is constant). The finish time computed by the integral is exactly the time the bundle would depart plus light delay. Thus the TEG edge matches the continuous transition. By induction over the hops, the composed TEG path achieves the same or earlier delivery. ∎

Lemma 2. Any path in the TEG from a start vertex to a destination vertex corresponds to a feasible continuous‑time forwarding plan with the same delivery time.

Proof. Waiting edges directly correspond to storage; contact edges exactly satisfy the volume requirement by construction. ∎

Together, the lemmas imply that the TEG faithfully represents the continuous‑time problem; an optimal solution in the TEG is an optimal solution in the original model.

