5. Shortest‑Delay Routing Algorithm

5.1 Dijkstra on the TEG

Assign to each edge a weight equal to the time difference between its endpoints. All weights are strictly positive (waiting edges have t_{k+1}-t_k > 0; transmission edges have total time > 0). The graph is acyclic? Not necessarily, but there are no negative cycles. We can therefore run Dijkstra’s algorithm to find the shortest (i.e., minimum‑delay) path from the start vertex to any vertex representing the destination node. The start vertex is (src, t_{\text{create}}) if t_{\text{create}}\in\mathcal{T}; otherwise we add it as a new instant and connect it with a waiting edge to the next instant.

The earliest delivery time is the minimum of t among all vertices (dst, t) reached, subject to t \le t_{\text{create}} + \ell. If no such vertex is reached within the lifetime, no feasible path exists.

Theorem 1. The Dijkstra algorithm on the TEG produces a path that achieves the globally minimal delivery time for a given bundle, provided that the contact capacities are constant on each interval between consecutive events in \mathcal{T} and that storage is unlimited.

Proof. The TEG captures all feasible state transitions. Dijkstra finds the shortest path with respect to the additive time metric. Since the edge weights are non‑negative, the algorithm correctly identifies the minimum‑time path. The correspondence lemmas guarantee that this path is realisable. ∎

5.2 Complexity and Practical Considerations

Let N = |V|, and let M be the number of contacts. The set \mathcal{T} contains at most 2M distinct times. Thus the TEG has O(N M) vertices. Each waiting edge adds O(N M) edges. For each contact and each TEG instant within its window, we create at most one transmission edge; the number of such instants is bounded by the number of intervals inside the window, which is O(M). Hence the total number of edges is O(N M + M^2). Dijkstra runs in O(E + V \log V), giving an overall complexity polynomial in the size of the contact plan.

In interplanetary networks, M can be on the order of thousands per week, but the planning horizon may be years, making the explicit TEG impractically large. Real‑world implementations such as Contact Graph Routing (CGR) and Schedule‑Aware Bundle Routing (SABR) approximate this search by building a forward‑looking route on demand, using Yen’s algorithm on a much smaller candidate contact set. Our formal TEG model provides the theoretical optimum against which these heuristics can be measured.

5.3 Incorporating Capacity Constraints on Contacts

The above construction assumes a bundle is the only traffic; in reality a contact’s volume is shared by many bundles. The TEG can be extended with a residual capacity label on each transmission edge: after a bundle is routed, the available volume on the contact’s edge is reduced by s, and edges that no longer satisfy V_{\text{remaining}} \ge s are removed. Successive routings then operate on an updated TEG. This sequential process is used in CGR’s capacity‑aware extensions; a full optimisation over multiple bundles is NP‑hard (it reduces to multi‑commodity flow with time windows), but greedy single‑bundle shortest‑delay allocation is the operational standard.

