6. Connection to Real DTN Protocols

Although this chapter has focused entirely on the mathematical model, every element maps directly to the IETF/CCSDS DTN architecture:

· The bundle tuple corresponds to the primary block of a BPv7 bundle (RFC 9171), with lifetime held in the primary block’s “expire time”.
· Contacts are the basis of the Contact Plan used by CGR and SABR; the contact volume integral is a core parameter in the CCSDS Schedule‑Aware Bundle Routing standard (CCSDS 734.2‑B‑1).
· The custodial storage represented by waiting edges is implemented through Bundle‑in‑Bundle Encapsulation (BIBE), which hands responsibility to the next custodian without any end‑to‑end ACK.
· The light‑delay \tau and capacity C are computed from orbital geometry and link budget equations exactly as in (1).

Thus the theoretical framework is not a hypothetical exercise—it is the mathematical foundation of the protocols that today carry data from the International Space Station, the Mars orbiters, and the upcoming Lunar Gateway.

---

7. Conclusion

We have presented a self‑contained mathematical model of scheduled delay‑tolerant networking, starting from the speed‑of‑light propagation delay and the Shannon–Hartley capacity bound, building through contact windows and bundle formalism, and arriving at a provably correct shortest‑delay routing algorithm based on time‑expanded graphs. Every definition is physically motivated, and the proofs are elementary but rigorous. This skeleton can serve as the theoretical core of any interplanetary network architecture, requiring no speculative elements to function—only the sound engineering of orbital schedules, radio links, and store‑carry‑forward discipline.

