Appendix A. Formal System Model — Complete Definitions

A.1 Time Domain

Time is continuous:  \mathbb{T} = \mathbb{R} . All events (bundle creation, contact start, signal propagation) are instantaneous points in  \mathbb{T} . For convenience we fix a global coordinate time, but relativistic corrections are ignored because node velocities  v \ll c ; the model is Newtonian.

A.2 Node Set

 V = \{1,2,\dots,N\} . Nodes have no internal processing delay; forwarding decisions are instantaneous. Each node can store bundles indefinitely.

A.3 Distance and Propagation Delay

The Euclidean distance between  i,j \in V  at time  t  is  d_{ij}(t) . We define the one‑way light‑time

\tau_{ij}(t) = \frac{d_{ij}(t)}{c},

with  c = 299\,792.458\ \text{km s}^{-1} . Because the contact plan is computed using ephemerides, we assume that for every scheduled contact  \tau_{ij}  is constant over the contact window and denoted by the single value at the start of the window. The maximum variation within a window (e.g., a few hours) is less than 0.1 ms even for Mars, well below protocol timer granularity; the assumption is justified for all practical purposes.

A.4 Signal-to-Noise Ratio and Channel Capacity

For a radio frequency link from  i  to  j :

· Transmitted power:  P_t  (W)
· Transmitter antenna gain:  G_t  (linear)
· Receiver antenna gain:  G_r  (linear)
· Carrier wavelength:  \lambda = c/f  (m)
· Boltzmann’s constant:  k = 1.380649\times10^{-23} \ \text{J K}^{-1} 
· System noise temperature:  T_{\text{sys}}  (K)
· Bandwidth:  B  (Hz)

The received power at distance  d  is

P_r = \frac{P_t G_t G_r \lambda^2}{(4\pi d)^2}.

Noise power is  N_0 = k T_{\text{sys}} B . The signal‑to‑noise ratio (SNR) is

\text{SNR} = \frac{P_r}{N_0} = \frac{P_t G_t G_r \lambda^2}{(4\pi d)^2 k T_{\text{sys}} B}.

Shannon’s theorem states that the maximum error‑free data rate (channel capacity) is

C = B \log_2(1 + \text{SNR}) \quad \text{(bits s}^{-1}).
\tag{A.1}

For optical communications the formula is identical, with  G_t,G_r  replaced by telescope gains and  T_{\text{sys}}  by photon shot noise; the quadratic inverse distance dependence remains.

A.5 Contact Definition

A contact from node  u  to node  v  is a tuple

\kappa = (u,\, v,\, t_{\text{start}},\, t_{\text{end}},\, C(\cdot),\, \tau),

where:

1.  t_{\text{start}}, t_{\text{end}} \in \mathbb{T} ,  t_{\text{start}} < t_{\text{end}} ;
2.  C : [t_{\text{start}}, t_{\text{end}}] \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}  is the instantaneous capacity function, assumed to be Riemann integrable;
3.  \tau = \tau_{uv}(t_{\text{start}})  is the constant one‑way propagation delay during the contact.

We denote the set of all contacts by  \mathcal{C} . The contact volume is

V(\kappa) = \int_{t_{\text{start}}}^{t_{\text{end}}} C(s)\,\mathrm{d}s.
\tag{A.2}

A.6 Bundle

A bundle is a quintuple

b = (s,\, src,\, dst,\, t_0,\, \ell),

where  s > 0  is the size in bits,  src,dst \in V ,  t_0 \in \mathbb{T}  is the creation time, and  \ell > 0  is the lifetime. The bundle must be delivered to  dst  by  t_0+\ell  or be discarded.

A.7 Feasible Forwarding over a Single Contact

Let a bundle  b  be present at node  u  at time  t_{\text{arr}} , and consider a contact  \kappa = (u,v, t_s, t_e, C, \tau) . Transmission is feasible iff

\int_{\max(t_{\text{arr}},\, t_s)}^{t_e} C(s)\,\mathrm{d}s \ge s.
\tag{A.3}

If feasible, the earliest time the transmission can finish (without pre‑emption of other traffic) is the minimal  \Delta \ge 0  satisfying

\int_{\max(t_{\text{arr}},\, t_s)}^{\max(t_{\text{arr}},\, t_s)+\Delta} C(s)\,\mathrm{d}s = s,

with the constraint that the transmission ends no later than  t_e . The bundle becomes available at node  v  at

t_{\text{dlv}} = \max(t_{\text{arr}},\, t_s) + \Delta + \tau.
\tag{A.4}

Note that if the integral equation has a solution, the transmission start can be delayed (e.g., to wait for a higher‑priority bundle) without affecting feasibility; the model finds the earliest possible delivery given that the bundle is transmitted as soon as capacity permits.

A.8 Multi‑Hop Path

A path  P  from  src  to  dst  is an ordered sequence of contacts

P = (\kappa_1, \kappa_2, \dots, \kappa_m),

with  \kappa_i = (n_{i-1}, n_i, t_s^{(i)}, t_e^{(i)}, C_i, \tau_i) ,  n_0 = src ,  n_m = dst , and the constraint that for all  i=1,\dots,m , the contact  \kappa_i  is feasible given the arrival time at  n_{i-1} . Formally, let  t_0^{(0)} = t_0  (bundle creation at  src ). For each hop  i , let  t_{\text{arr}}^{(i)}  be the time the bundle is available at node  n_{i-1}  (for  i=1 ,  t_{\text{arr}}^{(1)} = t_0 ; for  i>1 ,  t_{\text{arr}}^{(i)} = t_{\text{dlv}}^{(i-1)} ). The hop is feasible if

\int_{\max(t_{\text{arr}}^{(i)},\, t_s^{(i)})}^{t_e^{(i)}} C_i(s)\,\mathrm{d}s \ge s.

We compute the earliest transmission finish and the delivery time  t_{\text{dlv}}^{(i)}  as in (A.4). The path is feasible if all hops are feasible and  t_{\text{dlv}}^{(m)} \le t_0 + \ell . The delivery time of the path is  t_{\text{dlv}}^{(m)} .

Earliest‑Delivery Problem: Given  b  and  \mathcal{C} , find a feasible path that minimises  t_{\text{dlv}}^{(m)} , or report that none exists.

