Appendix B. The Time‑Expanded Graph (TEG) — Full Construction

B.1 Event Points

Let  \mathcal{T}_{\text{raw}}  be the set of all  t_{\text{start}}  and  t_{\text{end}}  from every contact in  \mathcal{C} . Sort them to obtain an increasing sequence  t_0 < t_1 < \dots < t_K . Define the discrete event points

\mathcal{T} = \{t_0, t_1, \dots, t_K\}.

If the bundle creation time  t_0  (the constant, not to be confused with the first event point) does not belong to  \mathcal{T} , we insert it as an additional event point and re‑index. For clarity we rename the bundle creation time  t_{\text{create}} .

B.2 Assumption: Piecewise Constant Capacity

For each contact  \kappa , the capacity function  C(s)  is constant on every interval  [t_k, t_{k+1})  that lies entirely within its window. Real contact plans provide capacity either as a single constant or as a sequence of fixed‑rate segments; the discretisation matches exactly. If a contact’s capacity changes continuously (e.g., due to changing distance), the planner must split the contact into sub‑contacts, each with constant rate. This is standard practice.

B.3 Graph Vertices

For every node  u \in V  and every event time  t_k \in \mathcal{T}  we create a vertex  (u, t_k) . The set of vertices is

V^* = \{(u, t_k) \mid u \in V,\; t_k \in \mathcal{T}\}.

B.4 Waiting Edges

For each node  u  and for each consecutive pair  t_k, t_{k+1}  we add a directed edge

(u, t_k) \xrightarrow{\text{wait}} (u, t_{k+1})

with weight  w = t_{k+1} - t_k . The edge indicates that a bundle can remain stored at node  u  during the whole interval.

B.5 Transmission Edges

For each contact  \kappa = (u,v, t_s, t_e, C, \tau) , let the event times that fall inside  [t_s, t_e]  be  t_p, t_{p+1}, \dots, t_q  with  t_p = t_s, t_q = t_e . For every departure event time  t_k \in \{t_p, t_{p+1}, \dots, t_{q-1}\}  (i.e., any event strictly before the contact end) we consider the possibility that the bundle starts transmission immediately at  t_k . Because capacity is constant on each sub‑interval, the transmission will occupy a whole number of intervals plus possibly a partial final interval.

Formally, we compute the minimal index  m \ge k  such that

\sum_{j=k}^{m-1} C \cdot (t_{j+1} - t_j) \ge s,
\tag{B.1}

with the understanding that if  m = k  the sum is zero. If such  m  exists and the cumulative interval end  t_m \le t_e , then transmission finishes at time

t_{\text{finish}} = t_m - \frac{1}{C} \left( \left[\sum_{j=k}^{m-1} C (t_{j+1}-t_j)\right] - s \right).
\tag{B.2}

Equation (B.2) subtracts the excess capacity in the last interval  [t_{m-1}, t_m]  to pinpoint the exact completion instant. Because  C  is constant, the volume transferred in the last partial interval is  s - \sum_{j=k}^{m-2} C (t_{j+1}-t_j) , so the time spent in that interval is  (s - \text{previous total})/C , and the finish time is  t_{m-1} + \text{that duration} . Expanding yields (B.2).

The bundle then propagates to node  v , arriving at

t_{\text{dlv}} = t_{\text{finish}} + \tau.
\tag{B.3}

We add a directed edge

(u, t_k) \xrightarrow{\kappa} (v, t_{\text{dlv}})

with weight  w = t_{\text{dlv}} - t_k . If the vertex  (v, t_{\text{dlv}})  does not already exist in  V^*  (i.e.,  t_{\text{dlv}} \notin \mathcal{T} ), we insert it as a new vertex and, for completeness, add waiting edges connecting it to the adjacent event times of node  v . In practice, this vertex will only be used as a destination for this transmission edge; it does not require outgoing waiting edges because a bundle that arrives at  t_{\text{dlv}}  can immediately be forwarded over a subsequent contact if one exists at that exact moment (which is unlikely but handled by inserting the vertex into the event timeline of node  v ).

B.6 Graph Properties

All edge weights are strictly positive (waiting time  t_{k+1}-t_k > 0 ; transmission time  > 0 ). The graph is finite if  \mathcal{C}  is finite. Because time always advances,  G^*  is acyclic? Not necessarily: waiting edges always go to later times, and transmission edges also have  t_{\text{dlv}} > t_k . So all edges go strictly forward in time; hence  G^*  is a directed acyclic graph (DAG). This guarantees that a simple topological ordering (or Dijkstra, which works on any graph with non‑negative weights) yields shortest paths without negative cycles.

