Appendix C. Correctness Proofs

C.1 Lemma 1: Every Feasible Continuous Path Maps to a TEG Path

Statement. Let  P  be a feasible path for bundle  b  (size  s ) in the continuous model, delivering the bundle to  dst  at time  T . Then there exists a directed path in the TEG  G^*  from vertex  (src, t_{\text{create}})  (if  t_{\text{create}}  is an event point; if not, from the first event point after  t_{\text{create}}  after appropriate insertion) to a vertex  (dst, t_{\text{dlv}}^*)  with  t_{\text{dlv}}^* \le T , and the total weight of this TEG path equals the actual delivery time in the TEG, which is  \le T .

Proof. We proceed by induction on the number of hops  m .

Base case  m=0 : The bundle never leaves  src . The continuous path trivially “delivers” at time  t_{\text{create}}  to  src  itself. The TEG contains vertex  (src, t_{\text{create}})  and a zero‑length path to itself; the delivery time is  t_{\text{create}} , which satisfies the inequality.

Inductive step: Assume the statement holds for paths of  m-1  hops. Consider an  m -hop path  P = (\kappa_1, \dots, \kappa_m)  with delivery time  T . By the feasibility of the path, there exist times:

·  t_{\text{arr}}^{(1)} = t_{\text{create}} ,
· For each hop  i=1,\dots,m : a transmission start  t_{\text{start},i} \in [\max(t_{\text{arr}}^{(i)}, t_s^{(i)}), t_e^{(i)}]  and a finish time  t_{\text{finish},i}  determined by the volume integral, with  t_{\text{dlv}}^{(i)} = t_{\text{finish},i} + \tau_i , and  t_{\text{arr}}^{(i+1)} = t_{\text{dlv}}^{(i)} .

We construct the TEG path incrementally.

Consider the first  m-1  hops. They form a feasible path delivering the bundle to node  n_{m-1}  at time  t_{\text{dlv}}^{(m-1)} . By the induction hypothesis, there exists a TEG path from  (src, t_{\text{create}})  to some vertex  (n_{m-1}, t^*)  with  t^* \le t_{\text{dlv}}^{(m-1)} . We can extend this path by adding waiting edges from  t^*  to the earliest event time  t_k \in \mathcal{T}  that is  \ge \max(t^*, t_s^{(m)}) . Because waiting edges cover all time intervals, we can advance the bundle’s presence to time  t_k  at node  n_{m-1}  without loss of feasibility: the remaining capacity in the contact  \kappa_m  from time  t_k  onward is at least what was used in the continuous plan, since waiting only delays the start and reduces the available window; the original continuous plan started at  t_{\text{start},m} , which is  \le t_k  after waiting, and the volume used from  t_k  to  t_{\text{finish},m}  is exactly the same as the continuous plan’s volume because the integration interval is shifted but the capacity function is constant on subintervals—the cumulative capacity from  t_k  to  t_{\text{finish},m}  equals  s .

Formally, in the TEG we are at vertex  (n_{m-1}, t_k) . The transmission edge for contact  \kappa_m  starting at  t_k  is present because the TEG construction checks the volume condition (B.1) using  t_k  as the departure event. Since the continuous plan had a feasible transmission starting at some  t_{\text{start},m} \ge t_k  (after our waiting), the condition holds: the integral from  t_k  to  t_e^{(m)}  is at least  s . Hence there exists a transmission edge from  (n_{m-1}, t_k)  to  (n_m, t_{\text{dlv}}')  with delivery time  t_{\text{dlv}}'  computed by (B.2)+(B.3). We need to show that  t_{\text{dlv}}' \le t_{\text{dlv}}^{(m)} .

In the continuous plan, transmission started at  t_{\text{start},m}  and finished at  t_{\text{finish},m} . In the TEG, we start at the (possibly earlier) event  t_k . Because capacity is constant on intervals, starting earlier at  t_k  means the same volume  s  is transferred by some time  t_{\text{finish}}' . The integral from  t_k  to  t_{\text{finish}}'  equals  s . The continuous plan’s transmission finished at  t_{\text{finish},m}  after starting at  t_{\text{start},m} \ge t_k . Because the rate function is non‑negative and constant on intervals, the time needed to transfer  s  from  t_k  is at most the time from  t_k  to  t_{\text{finish},m}  minus the idle time before  t_{\text{start},m} . More precisely, define  A = \int_{t_k}^{t_{\text{start},m}} C(t)\,\mathrm{d}t \ge 0 . In the continuous plan, the volume transferred from  t_{\text{start},m}  to  t_{\text{finish},m}  is  s . In our TEG, we transfer  s  total; hence we use some of the capacity in  [t_k, t_{\text{start},m}] , which only makes us finish earlier. The total volume transferred by the continuous plan up to time  t_{\text{finish},m}  starting from  t_k  is at least  s  (actually exactly  s  plus any unused capacity before  t_{\text{start},m} ). Therefore, by the time  t_{\text{finish},m} , we have certainly transferred  s  bits, so the TEG finish time  t_{\text{finish}}' \le t_{\text{finish},m} . Consequently  t_{\text{dlv}}' = t_{\text{finish}}' + \tau_m \le t_{\text{finish},m} + \tau_m = t_{\text{dlv}}^{(m)} . Adding the propagation delay and the waiting time, the total weight of the TEG path from  (src, t_{\text{create}})  to  (dst, t_{\text{dlv}}')  is at most the continuous delivery time  T . This completes the inductive step and the proof. ∎

C.2 Lemma 2: Every TEG Path Corresponds to a Feasible Continuous Plan

Statement. Any directed path in  G^*  from  (src, t_{\text{create}})  to a vertex  (dst, t_{\text{dlv}})  with total weight  W = t_{\text{dlv}} - t_{\text{create}}  can be realised as a feasible continuous multi‑hop forwarding plan delivering the bundle at time  t_{\text{dlv}} , provided that the bundle’s lifetime is not exceeded.

Proof. A TEG path consists of a sequence of waiting edges and transmission edges. Waiting edges correspond to time intervals where the bundle stays at the same node. We can break the path into segments: a waiting segment, then a transmission over contact  \kappa_{i_1} , then waiting at the next node, etc. For each transmission edge from  (u, t_k)  to  (v, t_{\text{dlv}})  labelled by contact  \kappa , the construction guarantees that the volume integral from  t_k  to  t_{\text{finish}}  equals  s  and that  t_{\text{dlv}} = t_{\text{finish}} + \tau . We define the continuous forwarding plan as:

· At each node, the bundle waits from its arrival time (which is a TEG vertex time) until the departure event time  t_k  of the next transmission edge.
· At time  t_k , transmission begins and runs until  t_{\text{finish}}  (continuous transmission exactly filling the capacity).
· The bundle then propagates over  \tau  and arrives at  v  at  t_{\text{dlv}} .

Because the waiting intervals match the TEG waiting edges and the transmission exactly fulfills the volume requirement, the plan is feasible in the continuous model and produces exactly the same timeline as the TEG path. Thus the TEG path yields a valid continuous plan with the same delivery time. ∎

C.3 Theorem 1 (Optimality of Dijkstra on TEG)

Statement. Let  G^*  be constructed as above from a finite contact plan  \mathcal{C}  with piecewise constant capacities, and let all edge weights be the time increments. Then running Dijkstra’s algorithm from the start vertex  (src, t_{\text{create}})  yields a shortest (minimum total weight) path to any reachable destination vertex. If a vertex  (dst, t^*)  is reached with minimum weight  t^* - t_{\text{create}} , and if  t^* \le t_{\text{create}}+\ell , then there exists a feasible continuous path delivering the bundle at time  t^* , and no feasible continuous path can deliver it earlier.

Proof. Because all edge weights are non‑negative, Dijkstra correctly computes the shortest path from the source to every vertex (Cormen et al.). Lemma 1 ensures that any continuous feasible path corresponds to a TEG path with delivery time  \le  the continuous one, so the optimal continuous delivery time cannot be smaller than the shortest TEG path weight to some  (dst, \cdot) . Conversely, by Lemma 2, the TEG shortest path yields a continuous feasible plan with exactly that delivery time. Hence the TEG shortest path time is precisely the infimum of feasible continuous delivery times. Dijkstra finds that minimum. ∎

