Appendix D. Complexity Analysis

D.1 Size of the TEG

Let  N = |V| , and let  M = |\mathcal{C}| . Each contact contributes two event times, so  |\mathcal{T}| \le 2M + 1  (including  t_{\text{create}}  if not present). Number of vertices:  |V^*| \le N \cdot (2M+1) . Additionally, each transmission edge may create a new arrival vertex; in the worst case, every combination of departure event and contact could create a distinct arrival time, but the number is bounded by  N \cdot (\text{number of transmission edges}) . A coarse bound: for each contact and for each event instant inside its window (up to  2M ), we create at most one transmission edge, so at most  M \cdot 2M = 2M^2  transmission edges, each potentially adding a new arrival vertex. So  |V^*| = O(NM + M^2)  and  |E^*| = O(NM)  (waiting edges:  N  per event interval, total  N \cdot (2M) ) plus  O(M^2)  (transmission edges). Overall  |V^*| = O(M^2 + NM)  and  |E^*| = O(M^2 + NM) .

D.2 Running Time of Dijkstra

With a binary heap, Dijkstra runs in  O(|E^*| + |V^*| \log |V^*|) . Substituting the bounds gives  O(M^2 + NM + (M^2+NM) \log(NM)) . For typical interplanetary networks with  N  small ( < 20 ) and  M  on the order of thousands,  M^2 \approx 10^6 – 10^7 , which is computationally acceptable for offline routing. Real‑time implementations use heuristics (CGR, SABR) that avoid building the full TEG.

D.3 Space Complexity

Storing the full TEG requires  O(M^2 + NM)  memory. This is manageable for planning horizons of months; for multi‑year continuous plans, pruning techniques and on‑demand graph expansion are used.

