Appendix E. From Physics to Contact Volume — An Explicit Calculation

We show how the contact capacity  C  and volume  V(\kappa)  are derived from physical parameters, connecting the mathematical model to the hardware layer.

E.1 Link Budget for a Mars–Earth Contact

Typical values for a Mars orbiter downlink at 2.7 AU (≈ 4.04 × 10⁸ km):

· Frequency: 8.4 GHz (X‑band),  \lambda = 0.0357\ \text{m} .
·  P_t = 20\ \text{W}  (13 dBW),  G_t = 40\ \text{dBi}  (high‑gain antenna).
·  G_r = 70\ \text{dBi}  (70‑m DSN dish).
·  T_{\text{sys}} = 25\ \text{K}  (cryogenic LNA).
· Bandwidth  B = 10\ \text{MHz} .

Free‑space path loss:

L = \left(\frac{4\pi d}{\lambda}\right)^2,
\quad d = 4.04\times 10^{11}\ \text{m},
\quad L \approx 2.0\times 10^{28} \approx 283\ \text{dB}.

Received power:

P_r = \frac{P_t G_t G_r}{L} = \frac{(20)(10^4)(10^7)}{2.0\times 10^{28}} = 10^{-16}\ \text{W} \ (-160\ \text{dBW}).

Noise power:

N_0 = k T_{\text{sys}} B = (1.38\times 10^{-23})(25)(10^7) \approx 3.45\times 10^{-15}\ \text{W}.

SNR:

\text{SNR} = \frac{P_r}{N_0} \approx \frac{10^{-16}}{3.45\times 10^{-15}} \approx 0.029 \ (-15.4\ \text{dB}).

Shannon capacity:

C = B \log_2(1+\text{SNR}) \approx 10^7 \times \log_2(1.029) \approx 4.2\times 10^5 \ \text{bps} = 420\ \text{kbps}.

Over a 4‑hour contact (14 400 s), the volume is

V = C \times 14\,400 \approx 6.0\ \text{GB}.

This matches real‑world Mars orbiter data return volumes.

E.2 Optical Link Example (Lunar)

A 1550 nm lasercom link between Earth and Moon (384 400 km) with 10 cm apertures yields SNR > 10 dB and capacities in the Gbps range; the same formulas apply. The TEG construction is identical.

