Appendix F. Connection to DTN Protocol Standards

The mathematical model is the theoretical underpinning of:

· BPv7 (RFC 9171): The bundle’s primary block contains the creation timestamp and lifetime (expire time). Custodial transfer is handled by the convergence layer; our storage edges model the “custody” state.
· SABR (CCSDS 734.2‑B‑1): Schedule‑Aware Bundle Routing uses a contact plan and capacity integrals identical to our definitions. The algorithm performs a greedy shortest‑path search that approximates the TEG optimum.
· CGR (Contact Graph Routing): The ION implementation builds a local contact graph and runs a Dijkstra‑like search on a time‑varying graph. Our TEG Dijkstra is the exact, offline version of that real‑time heuristic.
· BIBE (Bundle‑in‑Bundle Encapsulation): A convergence layer that uses the custody acceptance signals; our model abstracts these signals by assuming that once a transmission edge is taken, the bundle is reliably delivered to the next node (the physical layer handles error correction).

The gap between the theoretical optimum and practical heuristics is an active research area (e.g., CMR, SABR‑enhanced), where the TEG solution provides the golden benchmark.

